@meta
id: 25-07
title: An offset on a pattern
chapter: 25
type: lesson
skills: range-pattern, range-syntax
par: 21
minutes: 4
vscode: find the heading, click the line under it, select, delete
open: API.md

@brief
The refunds section of the API doc still carries the draft warning it was
written with. The endpoint shipped last sprint. Delete the warning line —
the one directly under the `## Refunds` heading. Every other section keeps
its own lines exactly as they are.

You cannot name that line by its text alone, because the same warning
appears under `## Disputes` and that one is still true. You can name it by
where it sits: one line below the heading.

Offsets attach to pattern addresses exactly as they do to `.` and `$`.

@keys
| `:/Refunds/+1d` | the line one below the next `Refunds` match |
| `/pat/+1` | one below the match |
| `/pat/-1` | one above it |
| `/pat/+` | `+` on its own means `+1` |

@hints
Find the line you can name — the heading — and then describe the target
relative to it.
--
The offset goes after the closing slash of the pattern.
--
`:/Refunds/+1d` Enter, then `:wq`.

@solution
    :/Refunds/+1d<Enter>  :wq<Enter>

Eighteen keystrokes and you never moved the cursor off line 1.

This is the address grammar paying off. An address is a *place*, and a
place can be found by searching, then adjusted by counting. Almost every
structured file has landmarks you can search for and targets you cannot:
the line under a heading, the line before a closing brace, the second
line of a fixture. `/landmark/±n` names all of them.

Both ends of a range can carry one:

    :/describe/+1,/});/-1>     indent the body of a test, not its edges
    :/BEGIN/+1,/END/-1d        the contents of a block, leaving the markers
    :?import?+1pu              put the register below the import above you

A pattern address with an offset still searches first and counts second,
so if the search fails, nothing happens and vim tells you `E486` rather
than guessing.

@start API.md
# Orders API

## Create order

POST `/orders`. Returns the created order with its generated id.

## Cancel order

POST `/orders/:id/cancel`. Issues a refund inside the thirty day window.

## Refunds
> Draft — this section describes an endpoint that does not exist yet.

GET `/refunds?orderId=`. Lists refunds for an order, newest first.

## Disputes
> Draft — this section describes an endpoint that does not exist yet.

POST `/disputes`. Opens a dispute against a settled payment.

@expected API.md
# Orders API

## Create order

POST `/orders`. Returns the created order with its generated id.

## Cancel order

POST `/orders/:id/cancel`. Issues a refund inside the thirty day window.

## Refunds

GET `/refunds?orderId=`. Lists refunds for an order, newest first.

## Disputes
> Draft — this section describes an endpoint that does not exist yet.

POST `/disputes`. Opens a dispute against a settled payment.
